{
  "dom_id": "step-q155565",
  "name": "Question 8",
  "problem": "Represent the solution of the inequality $4\\ge -3x+1$ on a number line.",
  "worked_solution": "First, we apply the addition principle, subtracting $1$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & \\ge -3x+1 \\\\ 4-1 & \\ge -3x+1-1 \\\\ 3 & \\ge -3x+0 \\\\ 3 & \\ge -3x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the multiplication principle, dividing by $-3.$ \n\nRemember, when multiplying or dividing by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\,\\, & \\ge \\,\\,-3x \\\\ \\frac{3}{-3}\\, & \\le \\,\\,\\frac{-3x}{-3} \\\\ -1\\, & \\le \\,\\,\\frac{\\cancel{-3}x}{\\cancel{-3}} \\\\ -1\\,\\, & \\le \\,\\,x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's swap the left and right-hand sides so that the variable is on the left-hand side. Remember, we also need to flip the inequality.\n\n$$\nx\\ge -1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\ge -1.$ The solution can be expressed on a number line, as shown below.\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14192536/assets/817328612514da56e2373483b7b579c5f8be03fb8b8ce182ec8220182a6bc9e7.png)",
